messages:
mei: FYI—we're seeing multiple onboarding tickets where users copy the quickstart and hit import/runtime errors. Seems like a mix of CJS/ESM, bundler behavior, and our package exports map.
frank: Can you paste the common errors?
mei: sure: "Named export 'InferenceClient' not found from '@redwood/inference'" and also "TypeError: Cannot read property 'InferenceClient' of undefined" from require() in some setups. A couple of reports mention Deno/deno bundle behavior and edge Runtimes too.
olga: Those errors usually mean the consumer's import style doesn't match the published module layout. We still have folks on Node CJS, others on ESM-only (edge), and then TS users without esModuleInterop.
frank: Proposed immediate fix: add a clear "Imports cheat-sheet" to README + tiny examples (examples/js/{cjs,esm,ts,edge}) with commands that actually run in CI. Copy/paste wins.
mei: Agreed. Draft snippets I want in README (short, explicit):
```
// Node CommonJS (package.json WITHOUT type: "module")
const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
// Redwood may be a namespace; client could be on Redwood.InferenceClient or Redwood.default?.InferenceClient
const client = new (Redwood.InferenceClient || Redwood.default?.InferenceClient)({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

// Node ESM or .mjs (type: "module")
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new Redwood.InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

// Strict ESM (edge runtimes / bundlers) - preferred
import { InferenceClient } from '@redwood/inference';
const client2 = new InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

// TypeScript (no esModuleInterop)
import Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
const clientTs = new (Redwood as any).InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
```

olga: Nice. Also add a tiny tsconfig hint so folks stop opening tickets: `{"compilerOptions":{"esModuleInterop":true,"allowSyntheticDefaultImports":true}}` in the docs.
frank: Can we also show a minimal package.json exports example explaining conditional exports? Something like this to guide advanced users and bundlers: 
```json
{
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./dist/esm/index.mjs",
      "require": "./dist/cjs/index.cjs"
    }
  },
  "main": "./dist/cjs/index.cjs",
  "module": "./dist/esm/index.mjs",
  "types": "./dist/index.d.ts"
}
```
mei: Great — that helps explain why bundlers choose one entry. Also call out edge: "Edge runtimes only accept ESM. Use named/ESM imports in serverless/edge examples."
eng-build: Operational notes: INF-342 opened to normalize publish outputs and ensure we emit both CJS and ESM artifacts + .d.ts with matching exports. Work items: update build pipeline, add package.json exports map, and add CI tests that run each example (node cjs, node esm, tsc compile, and a lightweight vercel/edge run).
support-bot: I've created an internal support playbook snippet linking to the README cheatsheet and examples: https://intranet.redwood/support/playbooks/js-sdk-imports. Use INF-342 as reference when escalating.
frank: Short term plan: 1) docs PR with cheat-sheet + explicit fallback patterns (show how to guard require() vs default vs named), 2) add examples/js/{cjs,esm,ts,edge} and CI smoke tests, 3) follow up on INF-342 for publish normalization.
mei: I'll open the docs PR and add the examples. If we can't change publish this sprint, docs+examples will reduce the tickets.
olga: One more note for the README: include a tiny troubleshooting section with quick commands users can run to inspect the package they installed: `node -p "require.resolve('@redwood/inference')"` and `node -e "console.log(require('@redwood/inference'))"` to show their runtime result. That helps support triage.
frank: Perfect. After docs land, update onboarding checklist so engineers see the multiple import patterns when creating sample apps. Closing thread once PR is linked.
